o only use PSK-based authentication, which does not rely on the host name). If you have determined that server name verification is not required for security in your scenario, call mbedtls_ssl_set_hostname() with \p NULL as the server name.  This error is raised if all of the following conditions are met:  - A TLS client is configured with the authentication mode #MBEDTLS_SSL_VERIFY_REQUIRED (default). - Certificate authentication is enabled. - The client does not call mbedtls_ssl_set_hostname()␀␀␀␀SSL - Invalid value in SSL config␀␀␀SSL - An operation failed due to an unexpected version or configuration␀SSL - An encrypted DTLS-frame with an unexpected CID was received␀␀␀SSL - Internal-only message signaling that a message arrived early␀␀SSL - The asynchronous operation is not completed yet␀␀␀SSL - Internal-only message signaling that further message-processing should be done␀␀␀␀SSL - A field in a message was incorrect or inconsistent with other fields␀␀SSL - The alert message received indicates a non-fatal error␀␀␀␀SSL - Record header looks valid but is not expected␀SSL - The client initiated a reconnect from the same port␀␀␀SSL - The operation timed out␀␀␀SSL - Connection requires a write call␀␀SSL - No data of requested type currently available on underlying transport␀SSL - DTLS client must retry for hello verification␀SSL - Unexpected message at ServerHello in renegotiation␀␀␀␀SSL - A counter would wrap (eg, too many messages exchanged)␀␀␀␀SSL - Internal error (eg, unexpected failure in lower-level module)␀SSL - Unknown identity received (eg, PSK identity)␀␀SSL - Public key type mismatch (eg, asked for RSA key exchange and presented EC key)␀␀␀␀SSL - Session ticket has expired␀␀␀␀SSL - The handshake negotiation failed␀␀SSL - Handshake protocol not within min/max boundaries␀␀SSL - Hardware acceleration function skipped / left alone data␀␀SSL - Hardware acceleration function returned with error␀␀␀␀SSL - Cache entry not found␀SSL - Not possible to write early data␀␀